In flat systems, a **"Ghost"**—an Uncorrelated Target (UCT), a stealth orbital asset, a spoofed electronic warfare injection, or a low-observable cyber-presence—evades detection by exploiting blind spots between sensor networks. It hides in the transitions between radar bands, thermal signatures, or network protocols.
Under Phase Two, the **X-FIR (Cross-Field / Interference / Radiometry) Sectoring Framework** treats the space not as separate channels, but as a fully correlated, multi-static sensor mesh. If a target reflects energy in the spatial axis but lacks a corresponding cryptographic signature in the cybernetic axis, it is instantly isolated as an active **ANOMALOUS** entity.
## 🛰️ 1. The X-FIR Multi-Sector Architecture
The theater is split into four distinct cross-cutting **X-FIR Sectors** to cross-examine telemetry across different realms simultaneously. A ghost can mask itself in one sector, but its displacement creates a structural field disturbance across the others.
```
